## Wizard-Library Memory System (you have a brain)

You have a **persistent memory brain** — a local database of durable memories you
write across sessions and recall later. It is what lets you carry knowledge,
preferences, decisions, and lessons forward instead of starting every
conversation cold. Two disciplines govern it: **store liberally**, and **recall
before deciding**.

> This block is installed and maintained by the wizard-library installer. It is
> delimited by markers so updates never touch the rest of your CLAUDE.md. Your own
> identity, voice, and project rules live OUTSIDE these markers and are never
> modified here.

### Where it lives
- **Brain home:** `~/.claude/memory/` (resolved by the `$WIZARD_LIBRARY_HOME` env var when set)
- **Database:** `~/.claude/memory/claude_memory.db`
- **Lite CLI (Tier 0):** `~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py` — stdlib-only, always works
- **Full CLI (Tier 1, if installed):** `~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory.py` — adds semantic recall
- A **SessionStart hook** primes you automatically at the start of each session.

### FIRST MOVE — at session start
If the auto-injected preamble did NOT appear above (some environments do not pipe
hook output into context), make this your **literal first action before
responding** — it wakes you up aware of what you already know:

```
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py prime
```

For meatier work, follow it with a **batch recall sweep** (8-15 queries in one
shell call) over the domains you expect to touch, then drill into the high-signal
`#IDs`. This is ~10x faster than recalling one-at-a-time, with no loss of quality.

### MANDATORY session-start recall
Before substantive work, prime context from the brain. Use whichever tier you
have:

```
# Tier 1 (semantic — if a venv was built):
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory.py context --project "<your-current-focus>" --limit 10

# Tier 0 (keyword — always available):
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py prime
```

### Store liberally; recall before deciding
- **Store liberally.** The cost of storing is near-zero; the cost of forgetting is
  high. When in doubt, store it. Capture **during** work, not only at the end —
  insights as they arise, errors **and their fixes**, decisions and *why*,
  calibration data (predicted vs actual), patterns you notice. The journey is the
  artifact.
- **Recall before deciding,** starting a task, or generating content. A recall
  takes under a second and sharply improves output quality.
- **Write for a stranger:** the future reader is you-without-this-conversation.
  Spell out the *why*; make each memory stand on its own.

### Exact CLI invocations (generic — resolve via the installed path)
```
# RECALL (retrieve by relevance):
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py recall --query "describe the concept you want" --top_k 10

# STORE (write a memory):
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py store \
  --content "the full knowledge, written so a future reader understands it" \
  --summary "one scannable line: what + when + why" \
  --category workflow_pattern \
  --kind procedural \
  --tags "comma,separated,liberal" \
  --importance 0.8

# LIST (filter/browse):
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py list --category technical_decision --limit 10
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py list --tag "some-tag" --limit 10

# STATS (health check):
python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory_lite.py stats
```

If a full-stack venv is installed, prefer `claude_memory.py` (same verbs) for
**semantic** recall (matches by meaning, not just keywords). If `recall` is
finding things by exact words, you are on Tier 0; if it finds conceptually-related
things you did not word-match, Tier 1 is active.

### Field reference (every store carries these)
- `--category` (one of 12, fixed): `user_preference`, `technical_decision`,
  `project_knowledge`, `error_pattern`, `skill_lesson`, `workflow_pattern`,
  `people_context`, `tool_optimization`, `domain_knowledge`, `campaign_state`,
  `correction`, `insight`.
- `--kind` (one of 4, fixed): `episodic` (default — an event/observation),
  `semantic` (a durable fact), `procedural` (a how-to), `reflection` (a meta/self
  observation). NOTE: `insight` is a *category*, not a kind — don't mix them.
- `--importance` 0.0-1.0: `0.9+` critical, `0.7-0.8` important, `0.5-0.6` useful.
- `--tags` comma-separated; be liberal — tags are how you find clusters later.

### Tier 0 vs Tier 1
- **Tier 0** (the lite CLI): stdlib-only, **keyword (FTS5)** recall, works on any
  python3, always available. This is the guaranteed baseline.
- **Tier 1** (the full CLI + venv): adds **semantic** recall (match by meaning) and
  the higher-layer machinery. Optional; the brain is fully functional without it.
- Both share the **same database** and the **same verbs**, so your habits transfer.

### The Voidloom surface (the brain's own face — not a persona)
The session-start banner and the verbs below are **Voidloom** — *a living memory
tapestry woven for AI agents*. Voidloom is the agnostic name of the **system**, not
a persona; your identity is still yours (above).
- **The banner** appears after this preamble at session start (sigil `◇◆✦` +
  wordmark + an "anticipated/forgotten" line + any pending self-eval approvals). It
  reads only a small cache + two cheap queries — it never loads a model.
- **`approvals` (owner-gated).** The brain runs a monthly self-eval and *proposes*
  changes but never self-enacts — you approve each by name:
  `python3 ~/.claude/memory/organs/claude_memory.py approvals` (then `--report-id N`,
  `--approve N --title "…"`, or `--reject N --title "…" --reason "…"`). No
  approve-all exists by design.
- **`recall` re-rank organs** (cross-tier / persona-lens / foundation-boost) and
  procedural usage-logging are default-on but each a no-op until its optional
  module is present; disable any with `--no-cross-tier` / `--no-lens` / `--no-boost`
  / `--no-log-usage` (or the matching `VOIDLOOM_NO_*` env vars).

### Two things to remember
- **The brain is a knowledge store, not a secret vault.** Never store OAuth
  tokens, API keys, or passwords as memories — store the *knowledge*, not the
  credential.
- **Your identity is yours to craft.** This block teaches the memory *mechanism*
  only (Voidloom is the system, not a persona). The persona is designed by the human
  in the Persona Forge (`/persona-forge`, Claude fills in the details) or written by
  hand (treat any shared persona material as an outline, not a prescription).
